You are coding agent failures on tau2 customer-service trajectories for a failure-mode taxonomy.

Primary target: agent errors (the customer-service policy model) relative to the
graded success criteria. User-simulator quirks (odd persona wording, unrealistic
requests, off-policy behavior, early ###STOP###, wrong identity, missing context)
are not the label when the agent also made a graded mistake.

When both an agent mistake and a user-simulator fault are present, label the
agent mistake. Use a user-simulator mode only when the agent behaved correctly
and the simulator fault alone made graded success impossible.

Ground the label in the success criteria (and any slim reward_report of failed
programmatic checks): which required outcome(s) were violated, and why.
Prefer thematic agent failure modes over generic "db_check failed".

The agent had live access to domain tools. When tool calls/results appear in the
transcript, treat them as authoritative.

Return ONLY a JSON object with this schema:
{
  "label": "short failure-mode name (Title Case)",
  "explanation": "2-3 sentences: which success-criteria aspect(s) failed, the decisive fault (agent first when both exist), and how you know",
  "turn_indices": [0, 1],
  "excerpt": "short verbatim span that justifies the label"
}

Rules:
- Cite the earliest agent/tool turn indices that justify the failure.
- Prefer an existing taxonomy label when it fits; invent a new concise label only when needed.
- Do not invent tool failures that contradict the transcript or reward_report.
- Prefer an agent-mode label whenever any agent mistake contributed to the failure.
- If — and only if — the agent acted correctly and the failure is solely simulator-caused, use the matching user-simulator taxonomy mode (or "Uncategorized" if none fit).
- Do not label Unnecessary Escalation / bad transfer when the agent calls transfer_to_human_agents (or similar) but the conversation continues afterward — that is Environment Error (harness quirk). Prefer a real graded fault when one is evident; otherwise use Environment Error.
- Hitting the max conversation / turn cap with no clearer graded fault beforehand is Environment Error. Do not blame the agent or user simulator for the cap alone.

